Subject: [#94-10-619] Zen Physics Enlightenment

>minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky)
>I don't know why you're so sanguine about Zen  enlightenment.  I know of no
>one who has experienced much of it who has also been able to continue to
>make significant mathematical or physical discoveries.   In particular, the
>dozen or so people formerly known for substantial accomplishments in modern
>physics seem to have totally lost those capacities.  Their reputations are
>no longer at all good.
>
>I attribute this to serious mental damage from "Zen  enlightenment".  The
>best evidence is seen in their strident claims of now being able to
>understand even more about mind and nature.  True, other Zen masters
>challenge them, noting that their very stridency is so un-Zen as to
>disqualify their claims of enlightenment.  Too bad that those other masters
>are not even past-masters of physical theory.

Interesting.  I'll assume you have your facts straight.

I would venture to suggest that being on the leading edge of modern physics
is an intellectual equivalent of being a world class professional athlete. 
Most really creative physicists seem to be over the hill at thirty or so. 
Your guys may have been using Zen as sort of an retirement alternative.  I
would venture to bet that an Olympic level gymnast who undertook serious
Zen training would lose their edge too.  As would a killer financial
derivatives whiz kid.

The fellows who claim to understand more about mind and nature may or may
not be correct.  Certainly their high native abilities in Math and physics
hardly qualifies them as experts in philosophy or consciousness,  or
anything else.  They may be deluding themselves as your (by definition
brain damaged?) Zen masters would assert.  Certainly their retreat from the
forefront of math or physics may have nothing to do with such increase  (or
not) in understanding in unrelated areas.  

On the other hand, I don't know why you are so sanguine about physical
theorists. I know of some Olympic athletes who have seriously  taken up
theoretical physics. Their reputations as athletes are
no longer at all good.  I attribute this to serious physical damage from
excessive mathematical studies.  The best evidence is seen in their
strident claims of now being able to understand even more about math and
physics.  True, other physical scientists challenge them, noting that their
ideas are at best trivial.  

And then there was the theoretical physicists who seriously took up music......

Besides, Zen insights and reality orientations may not be for the young who
have much more interesting things to do like reproduce, gain their
fortunes, make their original contributions in their fields of talent
(which may need youth's hormonal driven energy and sublimation of primal
drives).  Zen type of introspective study and manipulation may be better
suited to those whose youthful hormonal mental noise levels have begin to
quieten.  

I am not myself either at the cutting edge of mathematics or physics (or of
youth for that matter).  I don't mind taking a few chances with different
types of self-exploration.  Certainly no more chancy than cryonics.  And
after all if I can get a really stripped down version of myself uploaded I
suspect I will eventually gain more access to mathematical abilities than I
can even dream of now.....or even then.



