Subject: [#94-10-623] Argument by Analogy (Uploads)

L. Todd Masco wrote 10/30/94: <...our thoughts and feelings are the result of a
machine evolved to consider complex situations...>

I am not a machine.  Enlightenment determinism is a dried-out husk of ideational
detritus, yuck, ick and pew.

<Our entire method of evaluating ideas, for example, is frequently based first
upon an evaluation of the necessity of a fight-or-flight reaction because we
evolved in an environment where that's the highest priority task for survival.>

Something's not right here, the "entire method" implies that that is the only
way we do things, not just "frequently."  I would also say that since Descartes,
that is, since modern man, we can easily evaluate anything from totally within
our subjective self, without undo concerns for "fight-or-flight" mechanisms.

<When I'm uploaded, I'll be much closer to my ideal of considering ideas with no
prejudices...>

Umm....  Prejudices are often short-cuts that keep us from re-inventing the
wheel for every thought we have.  I don't think we should jettison this
mechanism too quickly.

<I define myself by continuity of experience and very little else.  I expect to
change, and hope to change in very basic radical ways.  That's what life is
about.>

Yes, life is largely biographical.

<..if we are able to toss out what we perceive to be our flaws...>

Shades of Utopia!  Yikes!  Oh brave new world that has such flawless uploads in
it!

<I also strongly suspect that, just like the abortion "when does human life
begin" question, there will never be any strongly agreed upon answer for what is
"conscious" and what is not.>

I have never been aware of any real argument over when life begins, obviously a
fertilized egg is life, no one has ever legitimately argued for any other
position.  The so-called "argument" has never amounted to more than a
smoke-screen for the death-worshippers to rationalize their pronounced penchant
for human sacrifice.  Likewise, I don't think there will be any legitimate
argument over what is conscious or not, there will only be smoke-screen
rationalizations for either bumping off conscious entities or extending phony
"rights" to nonconscious machines.

